WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.550
Reinforcement learning agents still learn far less
efficiently than humans,

00:00:04.630 --> 00:00:08.680
who freely borrow strategies from many sources and
rearrange them at will.

00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:12.810
This work, presented at NeurIPS 2023,

00:00:12.890 --> 00:00:16.940
introduces Knowledge-Grounded Reinforcement Learning,

00:00:17.020 --> 00:00:21.070
a paradigm that fuses multiple external knowledge
policies,

00:00:21.150 --> 00:00:25.200
and a new actor architecture called the
Knowledge-Inclusive Attention Network,

00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:29.330
or KIAN.

00:00:29.410 --> 00:00:33.460
KIAN lets an agent add, remove, and recombine
knowledge policies without retraining,

00:00:33.540 --> 00:00:37.590
and fixes an exploration pathology called entropy
imbalance,

00:00:37.670 --> 00:00:41.720
delivering more sample-efficient and flexible
learning across grid-world and robotics tasks.

00:00:42.100 --> 00:00:46.156
Reinforcement learning has succeeded across physics
and robotics,

00:00:46.236 --> 00:00:52.011
yet agents still need enormous numbers of samples
to solve tasks that humans master quickly.

00:00:52.091 --> 00:00:57.292
Part of the gap is that humans learn by observing
others and freely reuse, combine,

00:00:57.372 --> 00:00:59.965
and swap the strategies they already know.

00:01:00.045 --> 00:01:06.647
Earlier reinforcement learning methods did
incorporate external knowledge policies to improve efficiency,

00:01:06.727 --> 00:01:12.437
but they made it hard to perform arbitrary
combinations and replacements of those policies.

00:01:12.517 --> 00:01:16.510
That rigidity is exactly the property this paper
set out to fix.

00:01:16.890 --> 00:01:19.973
The authors summarize five properties of efficient,

00:01:20.053 --> 00:01:25.244
flexible human learning: being
knowledge-acquirable, sample-efficient, generalizable,

00:01:25.324 --> 00:01:27.166
compositional, and incremental.

00:01:27.246 --> 00:01:33.739
Existing knowledge-guided reinforcement learning
methods satisfy some of these but stumble on flexibility.

00:01:33.819 --> 00:01:38.886
When their fusion mechanism depends on the number
or ordering of external policies,

00:01:38.966 --> 00:01:44.777
rearranging the knowledge set or swapping one
policy for another means rebuilding or retraining

00:01:44.857 --> 00:01:46.328
large parts of the model.

00:01:46.408 --> 00:01:52.715
The motivation here is to design an actor whose
structure lets knowledge policies be freely rearranged,

00:01:52.795 --> 00:01:57.800
added, or replaced, so a single trained agent can
carry its skills into new tasks.

00:01:58.180 --> 00:02:01.864
The paper makes three main contributions.

00:02:01.944 --> 00:02:05.628
First, it defines Knowledge-Grounded Reinforcement
Learning,

00:02:05.708 --> 00:02:09.392
a paradigm that fuses an inner,

00:02:09.472 --> 00:02:13.157
self-learned policy with multiple external
knowledge policies.

00:02:13.237 --> 00:02:16.921
Second, it introduces the Knowledge-Inclusive
Attention Network, KIAN,

00:02:17.001 --> 00:02:20.685
whose embedding-based attention lets knowledge
policies be freely rearranged, added,

00:02:20.765 --> 00:02:24.449
or replaced without touching the rest of the network.

00:02:24.529 --> 00:02:28.213
Third,

00:02:28.293 --> 00:02:31.977
it uncovers a problem called entropy imbalance that
arises when maximizing entropy for exploration,

00:02:32.057 --> 00:02:35.742
proves when it happens, and proposes modified
policy distributions that fix it.

00:02:35.822 --> 00:02:39.506
Together these give an agent that is efficient,
generalizable,

00:02:39.586 --> 00:02:43.270
and truly modular in its use of knowledge.

00:02:43.650 --> 00:02:48.753
KIAN, the Knowledge-Inclusive Attention Network, is
built from three components.

00:02:48.833 --> 00:02:54.584
The inner actor is a normal learnable policy that
lets the agent develop its own strategy,

00:02:54.664 --> 00:03:00.480
so even if every external policy is useless for a
task the agent can still find a solution.

00:03:00.560 --> 00:03:03.525
Each knowledge policy, including the inner one,

00:03:03.605 --> 00:03:06.958
is given a learnable embedding vector called its key,

00:03:07.038 --> 00:03:11.429
which represents the whole policy independent of
any state or action.

00:03:11.509 --> 00:03:17.519
A state-dependent query then produces a vector that
is compared to every key by a dot product,

00:03:17.599 --> 00:03:21.147
and a softmax turns those scores into attention weights.

00:03:21.227 --> 00:03:26.071
The agent fuses all policies as a weighted sum and
samples the final action.

00:03:26.151 --> 00:03:31.189
Because each key is independent, the policies are
unordered, can be rearranged,

00:03:31.269 --> 00:03:34.688
and any one can be replaced just by replacing its key,

00:03:34.768 --> 00:03:37.020
without retraining the rest of KIAN.

00:03:37.400 --> 00:03:41.221
The experiments span two families of environments.

00:03:41.301 --> 00:03:45.122
MiniGrid provides discrete-action grid-world tasks
of increasing difficulty,

00:03:45.202 --> 00:03:49.023
from empty rooms to door-key puzzles, dynamic
obstacles, lava crossings,

00:03:49.103 --> 00:03:52.924
multi-room mazes, and key corridors.

00:03:53.004 --> 00:03:56.825
OpenAI-Robotics provides continuous-control
manipulation tasks such as Push, Slide,

00:03:56.905 --> 00:04:00.725
Pick-and-Place, and Reach.

00:04:00.805 --> 00:04:04.626
Crucially,

00:04:04.706 --> 00:04:08.527
every method starts from the same initial knowledge
set built from simple if-else programs

00:04:08.607 --> 00:04:12.428
that are deliberately sub-optimal and cannot
complete any task on their own.

00:04:12.508 --> 00:04:16.329
Each experiment is repeated with ten random seeds,

00:04:16.409 --> 00:04:20.230
and the reported learning curves show ninety-five
percent confidence intervals.

00:04:20.610 --> 00:04:22.560
Across both benchmark suites,

00:04:22.640 --> 00:04:30.052
KIAN was the only method to succeed in every
environment when starting from sub-optimal external knowledge,

00:04:30.132 --> 00:04:34.953
and its sample-efficiency advantage grew as tasks
became more complex.

00:04:35.033 --> 00:04:36.914
In zero-shot generalization,

00:04:36.994 --> 00:04:41.395
where a policy trained on one task is tested on a
different one,

00:04:41.475 --> 00:04:47.976
KIAN outperformed all baselines in most transfers
and did so with noticeably smaller variance.

00:04:48.056 --> 00:04:50.987
On the hardest simple-to-complex grid task,

00:04:51.067 --> 00:04:56.798
KIAN reached a reward of about 0.93 while the
strongest baseline stalled near 0.53.

00:04:56.878 --> 00:05:02.679
In continuous control, competing methods that
ignore the exploration issue collapse,

00:05:02.759 --> 00:05:05.480
whereas KIAN keeps learning efficiently.

00:05:05.860 --> 00:05:09.975
A key ablation isolates the paper's fix for entropy
imbalance.

00:05:10.055 --> 00:05:13.155
When KIAN runs with the original policy fusion,

00:05:13.235 --> 00:05:19.176
an agent maximizing entropy for exploration
collapses onto a single policy and struggles,

00:05:19.256 --> 00:05:23.980
especially on demanding tasks like dynamic
obstacles, multi-room mazes,

00:05:24.060 --> 00:05:26.348
and the robotic manipulation tasks.

00:05:26.428 --> 00:05:33.790
Switching on the modified policy distributions
restores efficient exploration and recovers strong performance.

00:05:33.870 --> 00:05:39.676
Separate compositional and incremental experiments
confirm the modular design pays off:

00:05:39.756 --> 00:05:45.765
KIAN reuses its learned keys and inner policy to
acquire new tasks sequentially with fewer

00:05:45.845 --> 00:05:48.810
samples than training each task from scratch.

00:05:49.190 --> 00:05:51.544
A few numbers capture the gains.

00:05:51.624 --> 00:05:56.488
In zero-shot simple-to-complex transfer on the
Empty-Random grid,

00:05:56.568 --> 00:06:04.398
KIAN scored about 0.93 at the sixteen-by-sixteen
size while the best competing method reached only 0.53.

00:06:04.478 --> 00:06:09.493
On the Pick-and-Place task tested at a
ten-times-larger goal range,

00:06:09.573 --> 00:06:13.905
KIAN reached 0.72 against 0.30 for the strongest baseline.

00:06:13.985 --> 00:06:19.001
In complex-to-simple transfer from DoorKey
eight-by-eight to Reach,

00:06:19.081 --> 00:06:25.161
KIAN achieved a perfect 1.0 versus 0.80 for the
reinforcement-learning baselines.

00:06:25.241 --> 00:06:30.333
All of this is measured across two benchmark
suites, five baselines,

00:06:30.413 --> 00:06:35.429
and ten random seeds with ninety-five percent
confidence intervals,

00:06:35.509 --> 00:06:39.460
and KIAN is the only method that succeeds everywhere.

00:06:39.840 --> 00:06:44.636
The lasting message is that treating each knowledge
policy as an independent,

00:06:44.716 --> 00:06:50.081
attention-addressable key turns external knowledge
into truly modular building blocks.

00:06:50.161 --> 00:06:51.538
An agent can add, drop,

00:06:51.618 --> 00:06:55.780
or reorder its policies at any time without
retraining the network,

00:06:55.860 --> 00:07:02.049
and the paper's fix for entropy imbalance keeps
exploration efficient when many policies are fused.

00:07:02.129 --> 00:07:07.368
The result is a reinforcement learning actor that
learns faster, generalizes better,

00:07:07.448 --> 00:07:08.571
and stays flexible,

00:07:08.651 --> 00:07:13.700
moving agents a step closer to the efficiency and
adaptability of human learning.
